 "notes": {
  "stairs3": "Joe (2026-09-28, on the round-two stairs): the tallest stair is on the far back left next to the projector, then a lower step at the back row of seats, another lower step at the middle row and the lowest step, to the ground, for the front row and the door. Then, on a first hand-painted version: artifacts beside the seats and an inconsistent join to the wall; make the black border on the wall step down in right angles like a stair profile (his reference: a flat stair-step silhouette), one horizontal section per level, the next just below it, with the blue light lines meeting the wall at the right angles. Round two's attempt g descended the right way but kept its strips at the old positions. Built by rounds/round-3-paint.py as geometry, replacing everything in the aisle: four levels 10 px apart (about a seat tier's end face); the black border keeps its painted 22 px height and steps down one level at each drop, its sections straight along the wall and its drops vertical, each drop's end given the border's rim; each drop's glowing turquoise edge runs along its row's tier-edge line (the row's lower edge carried up to the wall) from the right-angle corner where that line meets the upper level's floor line, corners at (15,514), (203,463) and (466,411); a 13 px shadow on the lower tread beyond each edge. Treads are carpet: a colour plane fitted to the plain carpet around the aisle, the floor's own departure from it carried in from the aisle's edge, and the carpet's fine pattern from open floor. The repaint reaches the seats' own edges (the mask is grown 4 px round the floor and only floor-coloured pixels are repainted there), so no old floor is left beside them."
 }
